
{%- liquid
  unless variant
    assign variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  endunless
  assign current_price = variant.price
  assign compare_price = variant.compare_at_price
  # Free product shortcut — show "Free" label in place of $0.00
  # and suppress the sale badge entirely. Without this guard a
  # variant priced at 0 with compare > 0 emits a "-100% OFF" badge
  # over a "$0.00" which reads as broken (sale mechanics against
  # zero are nonsensical — either it's a giveaway or bad data).
  assign is_free = false
  if current_price == 0
    assign is_free = true
  endif
  assign on_sale = false
  if compare_price > current_price and is_free == false
    assign on_sale = true
    assign discount_pct = compare_price | minus: current_price | times: 100 | divided_by: compare_price | round: 0
  endif
-%}

<div class="kt-product-price" data-product-price>
  <span class="kt-product-price__current{% if on_sale %} kt-product-price__current--sale{% endif %}">
    {%- if on_sale -%}
      <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'kt.product.sale_price' | t }}&nbsp;</span>
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if is_free -%}
      {{ 'kt.cart.free' | t | default: 'Free' }}
    {%- else -%}
      {{ current_price | money }}
    {%- endif -%}
  </span>

  {%- if on_sale -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      Compare-at renders inside <s> so screen readers announce the
      strike-through as a semantic "no-longer-valid price". A CSS
      text-decoration alone doesn't convey the meaning to AT.
      Visually-hidden prefix gives the reader the label, so AT
      reads "Regular price $80, sale price $60" instead of "80 60".
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <s class="kt-product-price__compare">
      <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'kt.product.regular_price' | t }}&nbsp;</span>
      {{ compare_price | money }}
    </s>
    <span class="kt-product-price__discount">
      -{{ discount_pct }}%
    </span>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    `data-template` carries the localized "X per Y" copy so the JS path
    can re-render the suffix without re-fetching the page or parsing
    the original Liquid filter at runtime.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- render 'product-unit-price', product: product, variant: variant -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    `cart.taxes_included` is true when Shopify Admin's "All prices
    include tax" toggle is on; it's false in tax-exclusive markets
    (e.g. US default), which renders this block as nothing — no
    misleading "Tax included" label on a price that doesn't actually
    include tax.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if cart.taxes_included -%}
    <span class="kt-product-price__tax-note">
      {{ 'kt.product.taxes_included' | t | default: 'Tax included.' }}
    </span>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    `quantity_price_breaks_configured?` short-circuits when no
    breaks exist (the common case for non-B2B stores), so the block
    renders nothing on retail storefronts. Each break renders the
    minimum quantity threshold + the per-unit price.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    `data-volume-pricing` is the JS anchor. The UL ships in the DOM
    server-side for the initial variant; kt-product-form.js's variant-
    change handler rebuilds the <li> children from the matched
    variant's `quantity_price_breaks` array. When a new variant has
    no breaks (retail variant of a mixed-catalog product), the UL
    is hidden via `hidden`. Always-render the wrapper so the JS
    target exists even on B2B variants that come back later.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <ul
    class="kt-product-price__breaks"
    aria-label="{{ 'kt.product.volume_pricing' | t | default: 'Volume pricing' }}"
    data-volume-pricing
    {% unless variant.quantity_price_breaks_configured? and variant.quantity_price_breaks.size > 0 %}hidden{% endunless %}
  >
    {%- if variant.quantity_price_breaks_configured? and variant.quantity_price_breaks.size > 0 -%}
      {%- for vbreak in variant.quantity_price_breaks -%}
        <li class="kt-product-price__break">
          <span class="kt-product-price__break-qty">
            {{ 'kt.product.volume_qty_html' | t: qty: vbreak.minimum_quantity }}
          </span>
          <span class="kt-product-price__break-price">{{ vbreak.price | money }}</span>
        </li>
      {%- endfor -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </ul>
</div>
